add(0,'approved',125,327,['f1'],[5,3,5,4,4,4,4],
"Read FcWdzqCpj5c captions 94–378 and the catalogue: embeddable; Creating Settings class is chapter 127–345. At 145–177 the documentation explanation says inheriting BaseSettings reads unspecified fields from the environment; at 186–215 the creator imports BaseSettings from pydantic_settings, declares Settings and a string app_name with a default. At 219–250 Settings is imported, instantiated and accessed in a route. At 289–327 a command supplies APP_NAME and the endpoint prints the changed value. This supports f1 against the official settings example. Do not confirm f2: no missing-required or invalid-value failure is taught; no dotenv precedence instruction supports f3. The pre-existing single route needs only allowed path-operation knowledge, not later dependency or logging knowledge. ADVISORY boundary repair: start at 125 to retain the transition into config.py, and end at 327 after the demonstrated override and explicit environment-reading conclusion; this avoids the longer deployment recap and later complex types. Editor, documentation and terminal cues support useful code visuals, not independently verified annotations.")
add(1,'approved',0,225,['f1'],[5,3,5,4,4,3,4],
"Read ZqPiHAECp68 captions 0–259 and its catalogue (embeddable). The introduction explains configuration and installing pydantic-settings; at 74–85 it correctly orders constructor values, environment and dotenv values. At 85–127 the creator imports BaseSettings from pydantic_settings, subclasses it and declares three string fields. At 138–197 constructing Settings produces three missing-field errors, then supplying app_name leaves two errors. At 197–225 the shell script is explained as putting values into the environment, and running Settings prints the loaded values. This is a standalone typed-settings demonstration supporting f1. ADVISORY fact reduction: omit proposed f2 from confirmed facts because the range demonstrates missing fields but never invalid-type/value validation; every declared field is a string. The opening mentions startup configuration but does not demonstrate fail-before-serving integration. Shell sourcing is explained sufficiently to follow the example; password/port examples do not require later authentication or serving knowledge. Keep the introduction and cut at 225 before references to validators in previous videos. Terminal failures and class edits provide visual value; later legacy dotenv configuration is outside the cut.")
add(2,'approved',235,368,['f1','f2','f3'],[5,4,5,4,4,4,4],
"Read qAh5dDODJ5k captions 201–398 and catalogue chapters Advanced Usage with Client (235–327) and Async API (327–380); embedding is enabled. At 242–271 the speaker contrasts a new connection per top-level request with pooled Client connections. At 271–309 the context-managed client is reused across a hundred-request loop. At 328–351 the speaker explicitly transfers the pattern 'in a similar way' to async with httpx.AsyncClient() and awaited get/post; at 351–368 a locally defined async function is run and returns a response. Together this teaches f1, scoped reuse f2 and context-managed ownership f3, consistent with the HTTPX async documentation. The reuse loop is synchronous groundwork, not an independently demonstrated async loop; cleanup is taught through the async-with pattern, not explicit aclose or a verbal scope-exit explanation. Allowed cooperative async and context-managed cleanup make both understandable. ADVISORY boundary repair: extend the end from 365 to 368 so 'this is how to do it in an async way' finishes, before the speed-comparison preview and web-app-testing topic. Code and execution cues support visual teaching without asserting independently viewed frames.")
add(3,'approved',85,345,['f1','f4'],[5,3,5,4,4,3,4],
"Read WRjXIA5pMtk captions 52–378 and its embeddable catalogue, including Creating single post endpoint (84–415). At 85–135 path values are introduced as URL variables captured as handler arguments and validated using type hints. At 159–194 the route receives {post_id} and post_id: int. At 194–229 the in-memory list of dictionaries and matching-ID loop are explained inside the cut. At 258–302 the explanation repeats brace binding and says /hello produces a validation error because hello is not an integer. At 319–345 browser requests show IDs one and two returning the matching post. Confirm f1 and f4; f4 is explained rather than demonstrated with an invalid request. Query binding and defaults f2/f3 are absent. There is no blocked prerequisite needed to follow the new route or basic list lookup. The temporary not-found dictionary is explicitly disclaimed as incorrect at 244–253 and 309–317, so it is not presented as the correct HTTP-error pattern. ADVISORY: retain that warning in accompanying text. Extend the end one second to 345 to finish the second successful lookup, before the missing-resource test; narrated editor/browser interaction provides visual value.")
add(4,'rejected_fact_mismatch',None,None,[],[5,2,5,4,4,3,3],
"Read WRjXIA5pMtk captions 593–791 and catalogue Testing path validation (630–755); embedding is enabled. At 632–660 the existing integer path input is tested with hello. At 660–689 the browser error and server output establish status 422, and the speaker reads 'Input should be a valid integer, unable to parse string as an integer.' However, the range never identifies detail entry loc or type fields and never makes the required test assertion. At 694–759 it instead switches to generated documentation, valid and missing-resource requests, and a Swagger input widget that refuses hello client-side. That refusal is not an additional server-generated validation response. BLOCKING: the sole claimed fact f2 includes asserting the default invalid-input status and inspecting detail entries for location and error type; the captions verify only part of it, and catalogue/thumbnail metadata cannot verify those unspoken displayed fields. The official path-parameter example explicitly contains loc and int_parsing, which the range does not explain. Reject rather than certify full f2 or invent screen evidence. Replace with a segment that actually walks through those fields and the invalid-input assertion; trimming this range cannot add them. Prior typed-path and HTTP-error knowledge is allowed, so this is not a prerequisite rejection.")
add(5,'rejected_fact_mismatch',None,None,[],[4,2,5,3,4,4,2],
"Read 7MHDDOrDx-w captions 133–336 and catalogue Exception handlers (169–303); embedding is enabled. The start at 165 is the end of an earlier hierarchy explanation ('other types of errors ... separately'). At 176–229 the creator points to an already-written createExceptionHandler factory accepting a status code and initial detail, handling SkyPos API errors, returning a JSON response and registering handlers for specific classes. At 229–235 there is a sensitive-data warning; at 240–287 a service-unavailable exception is raised and the resulting error is shown in Swagger, then logs are inspected through 304. BLOCKING: the only proposed fact f1 requires the handler's request-and-exception interface and JSONResponse(status_code=..., content=...) stable public structure. Neither the handler request parameter nor its returned content structure is established by the captions; a reference to an existing factory and a resulting message is insufficient to verify them, and catalogue/thumbnail cues do not supply the missing code evidence. The official custom-handler example explicitly supplies request, exc, status_code and content. Reject for incomplete claimed-fact support rather than infer unseen keywords. Replace with a self-contained handler-definition walkthrough. The start could be fixed near 170, but that does not recover the missing fact. Router and domain-error knowledge are allowed; the logger mention is not a blocked prerequisite.")
add(6,'approved',193,254,['f2'],[4,3,5,5,3,4,4],
"Read 2JPDt-Jp6fM captions 161–284 and catalogue Async and FastAPI basics (63–285); embedding is enabled. At 193–209 a fresh explanation explicitly returns to route definitions in main.py. At 206–228 normal def routes are described as running automatically in a separate thread pool so other requests can continue. At 228–251 async def routes are described as executing on the main event loop, with blocking I/O stopping that loop. This supports f2 against FastAPI's path-operation documentation, independent of the earlier series application. Do not add f1 or f3: library-specific selection and the direct ordinary-utility-function dispatch distinction are not taught. ADVISORY: the blanket wording 'must await for any I/O' should be contextualized as using awaitable I/O; merely adding await does not make a blocking library asynchronous. Extend the end to 254 to complete 'worse than just using a regular def function,' rather than ending at 251 mid-caption. References to the open route code provide modest but relevant visual support for a genuinely conceptual explanation; no drawn execution trace is verified. This judgment is limited to the approved range, not an endorsement of the later database conversion sections.")
add(7,'approved',67,145,['f1','f2'],[5,4,5,5,3,4,4],
"Read NkgIHa6KtHg captions 33–177 and all catalogue chapters; embedding is enabled. Catalogue timing is not reliable here: the supplied Project structure setup chapter begins at 97, whereas captions 98–144 still explain the router mechanism. At 67–98 the creator announces reorganizing into separate user/post route files and including them from main.py without changing external URLs. At 98–137 APIRouter is defined, router = APIRouter(), router.get/router.post replace app.get/app.post, and app.include_router connects the feature router to the application. This conceptual pattern supports f1 and f2 against the official bigger-applications examples. It does not fully support f3: prefix/tags are named, but path concatenation, dependencies and responses are absent. Flask blueprints are an optional analogy, not knowledge needed for the following definition; prior database handlers need not be understood. ADVISORY boundary repair: start at 67 instead of 66, omitting the tail 'nightmare to maintain', and end at 145 after keeping related code together, before the directory-creation transition. Main.py/editor and decorator references supply moderate relevant visual value, not verified annotations.")
add(8,'approved',323,534,['f1','f3'],[4,4,5,4,4,3,4],
"Read f270BoTicMA captions 287–570 and catalogue Implementing DI in FastAPI (289–473) / Advanced DI examples (473–802); embedding is enabled. At 327–351 FastAPI/Depends imports, an app and a simple Logger class are introduced locally. At 351–415 constructing the logger inside each endpoint is contrasted with shared provision logic. At 415–463 get_logger returns a new Logger and Depends supplies that object to the handler. At 473–534 the creator imports Annotated, declares an Annotated[Logger, Depends(get_logger)] alias and uses that handler type instead of repeating the declaration. These support f1 and f3 against the official dependencies example; f2's reason for passing rather than calling the provider is not explained. A Logger whose sole operation prints is understandable without blocked structured-log/redaction knowledge, and query inputs are allowed. ADVISORY: the 463–473 promotion interrupts teaching, but does not dominate it. The language 'not ... instantiate an object every single time' at 527–534 means avoiding manual repeated construction in handlers only: the shown provider still explicitly returns a new object, so accompanying text must not infer application-wide singleton reuse. Keep the 534 end after 'for us', before the next email-service implementation. Code-edit cues support visual teaching; no actual HTTP request/result is demonstrated in this range.")
add(9,'approved',1514,1562,['f3'],[5,3,5,5,3,4,4],
"Read 4HxjBvZMAg8 captions 1478–1596 and catalogue Defining request schemas (1440–1563) / Implementing API endpoints (1563–2263); embedding is enabled. The actual conceptual transition occurs at 1514, despite being within the schemas chapter. At 1514–1538 background tasks are introduced using the wait for sending email as a concrete reason to return promptly. At 1538–1551 the speaker states that FastAPI runs the task after the response is sent and pending tasks are lost if the server crashes; at 1549–1563 critical work is contrasted with a task queue, and a user can request another reset email. This clearly teaches f3's lack of durable delivery, consistent with the FastAPI same-process caveat. f1's parameter/add_task declaration and f2's callable forms are not taught. Earlier password-reset schemas and later database operations are not needed to understand this explanation; Celery is only an alternative named in passing. The short, concrete response-first/crash-loss explanation gives strong standalone conceptual clarity even though editor visuals are only modest support and no execution diagram is verified. ADVISORY boundary repair: extend the end to 1562 to finish 'request another one' before the endpoint-implementation transition, rather than cutting at 1561.")
add(10,'rejected_weak_visual',None,None,['f1'],[5,3,5,4,1,4,3],
"Read PQngw-cqVy4 captions 28–123 and catalogue How to prevent SSRF vulnerabilities (60–248); embedding is enabled. At 60–74 URL validation is introduced; at 74–93 the speaker says to check an approved whitelist when destinations are known, with images fetched only from a particular server as the example, limiting requests to that IP address or hostname. This accurately supports f1 against OWASP's identified/trusted-destination allowlist case. f2/f3/f4 are not taught in the proposed cut. The 91 end actually clips 'that host name' (caption 89–93); ending near 93 could fix that. ADVISORY quality rejection: this talking-head-only proposal has no code, browser, diagram or policy-check visual cues in its captions, and its broad whitelist recommendation is useful but not exceptionally explanatory enough to justify the pure-talking-head exception. Catalogue prevention-chapter and generic thumbnail metadata do not establish visual teaching support. A later blacklist discussion cannot supply visual value to this range. Seek a visually supported destination-policy mechanism or a substantially clearer standalone conceptual explanation; do not fabricate a visual mode. This is a nonblocking media-quality rejection, not an accuracy or prerequisite defect, and f1 remains independently confirmed as content spoken in the rejected range.")
